<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National University of Córdoba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1613">1613</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as University of Córdoba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> is the oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in Córdoba in 1613 and given university status in 1622. After the expulsion of the Jesuits in 1767 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> and later to the state. In 1918 its students launched the University Reform movement, which spread university autonomy across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-university-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="National University of Córdoba" width="320" height="112" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unc_univ_nac_cordoba_textlogo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1610 the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> founded the Collegium Maximum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, attended by students of the order, which was the precursor of the university. Under the administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Tucumán, Juan Fernando de Trejo y Sanabria, advanced studies began at what became known as the Colegio Maximo de Córdoba, but it could not confer degrees. Pope Gregory XV granted that authority on 8 August 1621, and the document reached the city in April 1622. With that authorisation, and the approval of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> provincial Pedro de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a>, the university began its official existence. It was the third oldest university of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, after San Marcos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> (1551) and Saint Thomas Aquinas University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> (1580).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> controlled the university until King Carlos III expelled them in 1767, after which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> took over. For its first 150 years it studied only philosophy and theology. At the end of the 18th century law was added, and a conflict between the Franciscans and the secular clergy led to a royal decree renaming it the Royal University of Saint Charles and Our Lady of Monserrat, with secular clergy in charge under Monsignor Gregorio Funes. Funes introduced far-reaching reforms and new subjects. The new regime of the May Revolution of 1810 took control of the university, and he stayed in his post.</p><p>The university was nationalised in 1856. Theological studies were eliminated in 1864, and under President Faustino Sarmiento foreign lecturers helped open the School of Exact, Physical, and Natural Sciences, with an Academy of Exact Sciences and an Astronomical Observatory. The School of Medicine opened in 1877, and the Law of Avellaneda of 1885, the first law on universities, set the framework for national universities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1918 the university was the scene of the University Reform. Students inspired by the ideas of autonomy, co-governance and democratisation of education led a movement that spread throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and had a lasting effect on higher education. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> has been nicknamed La Docta, the Wise. The College of Monserrat, the original buildings of the university and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> church form the Jesuit Block, declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2000.</p><p>The university is public and autonomous, with its own budget, administration and regulations, and since the early 20th century it has been the second largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. Alumni include the presidents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-de-la-rua">Fernando de la Rúa</a> and Héctor Cámpora. In 1952 a campus, the University City, was established in the south of the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National University of Córdoba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jesuits founded the <b>University of Córdoba</b> in 1613, the oldest in Argentina, and ran estancias in the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-tucuman">Governorate of Tucumán</a></li></ul></section>
